# 4. BAT-BALL PHYSICS AND SHOT OUTCOME BALANCING

The current biggest gameplay issue is that nearly every connected ball flies too far.

This must be corrected.

A successful bat-ball collision must not automatically produce a boundary.

## 4.1 Real physical contact

Resolve the outgoing ball velocity using:

- Incoming ball velocity.
- Ball spin and seam state.
- Bat linear and angular velocity.
- Bat swing direction.
- Bat-face orientation.
- Contact position on the blade.
- Contact height.
- Timing error.
- Footwork and body balance.
- Shot intent.
- Incoming pace and movement.
- Relative impact velocity.
- Bat-ball restitution and tangential friction.

Use actual collision geometry and impulse calculations.

Do not use a fixed exit velocity assigned by the shot button.

Do not make every connected hit a powerful lofted shot.

Keep the ball's energy and momentum behavior consistent with the simulation model. Tune parameters through testing rather than applying unexplained multipliers.

## 4.2 Create genuinely different shot results

A player must be able to produce:

- Low ground drives.
- Soft defensive pushes.
- Controlled singles.
- Firm shots into field gaps.
- Ground strokes reaching the rope.
- Lofted shots.
- Mistimed aerial shots.
- Top edges.
- Inside edges.
- Outside edges.
- Deflections towards the wicketkeeper or slips.
- Shots that stop short of fielders.
- Shots that are intercepted and returned.

The same successful contact must not always produce approximately the same launch angle, speed or boundary distance.

A good connection can still be stopped by a correctly positioned fielder.

A well-directed shot into an open gap should be rewarded.

A weak or poorly directed shot can become a catch or dot ball.

## 4.3 Four and six rules

Determine boundaries from the ball's actual world-space trajectory.

- SIX: Ball crosses the boundary in the air without first touching the ground.
- FOUR: Ball reaches the boundary after touching the ground.
- Grounded drives and placements must be capable of stopping inside the field.
- Fielders may intercept a grounded ball before it crosses the boundary.
- A fielder may save a boundary by intercepting, gathering or parrying the ball while respecting boundary contact rules.

The LOFT button must never guarantee a six.

A perfectly timed shot must not guarantee a four.

The trajectory and actual field geometry decide the scoring result.

