Advanced shots must only work when the incoming delivery actually occupies a reachable contact corridor.

For example:
- A ramp against a suitable short delivery may deflect the ball over the keeper or slip cordon.
- An attempted ramp against an unreachable wide ball must produce a miss or edge.
- A front-foot cover drive against a short bouncer must not magically become a successful cover drive.

## 5.2 Shot-to-delivery suitability

Use the following as cricket logic and animation guidance.

| Shot | Suitable situation | Main risk |
|---|---|---|
| Forward defence | Full/good-length ball that can be met on the front foot | Inside/outside edge, LBW or bowled if beaten |
| Back-foot defence | Shorter ball reachable from the back foot | Bounce variation, edge or miss |
| Straight drive | Full ball near the stumps | Bowled/LBW if bat arrives late; mistimed loft |
| Cover/off drive | Full or slightly wide ball outside off | Outside edge or cover/point catch |
| Square drive | Width outside off with front-foot access | Edge or aerial catch if reaching |
| On drive | Full ball near middle/leg | Leading edge or inside edge |
| Flick/glance | Ball directed towards pads/leg side | LBW, bowled or mistimed placement |
| Cut/square cut | Short and wide outside off | Top edge or point catch |
| Late cut | Wider ball passing the batter later | Keeper/slip edge |
| Pull | Short ball around chest height | Top edge, mistiming or deep catch |
| Hook | Short ball around upper-body/head height | High catch and high execution risk |
| Sweep | Full/low ball, especially against spin | Top edge, LBW or bowled |
| Paddle sweep | Low ball suitable for fine placement | Fine catch or edge |
| Reverse sweep | Suitable spin/full-length ball | Miss, top edge or stumping risk |
| Lofted drive | Full ball inside the hitting arc | High catch risk |
| Ramp/scoop | Delivery reaching the specific scoop corridor | Miss, edge or catch |
| Uppercut | Short, wide ball outside off | Top edge or third-man/slip catch |

Do not allow a shot-family name to force an outcome.

The physical trajectory and contact point must remain authoritative.

## 5.3 Footwork

Support:

- Front Foot.
- Back Foot.
- Advance/Charge.
- Balanced/Neutral.
- Leave.

Footwork must change the batter's actual position, balance, contact plane and swing reach.

Front-foot and back-foot movements must not be identical with different labels.

The advance action must move the batter down the pitch and create genuine stumping risk if the ball passes the bat and the batter remains out of the crease.

Leave must withdraw the bat from the ball's intended corridor and maintain the appropriate body posture.

## 5.4 Preserve true 360-degree placement

The existing joystick remains continuous.

Its vector must influence the bat swing, body alignment and bat face.

All ground sectors and behind-the-wicket sectors must be reachable.

However, the joystick expresses intention, not a guaranteed direction.

Incorrect alignment and contact must be capable of producing slices, edges, pulls, hooks, mistimed contact or misses.

A direction selected by the player must never silently be changed into an easier shot.

