# 11. REALISTIC SHOT SCORING AND GAME DIFFICULTY

Balance both sides.

The objective is not to make batting extremely difficult or make every delivery a wicket.

The objective is to make every delivery a competitive decision.

## 11.1 Expected result variety

The simulation must support meaningful variation across:

- Dot balls.
- Singles.
- Twos.
- Threes.
- Fours.
- Sixes.
- Bowled.
- Caught.
- LBW.
- Run out.
- Stumped.
- Hit wicket.
- Extras where applicable.

Do not hardcode a percentage of deliveries to become wickets or boundaries.

Outcomes emerge from delivery, batting, fielding, running and rules.

## 11.2 Correctly rewarding skilled batting

A correctly selected shot, suitable footwork, good timing and clean contact should receive a meaningful advantage.

If the player finds a genuine gap and strikes the ball well, the result should reward that decision.

However:
- A fielder may stop a well-struck ground ball.
- A straight drive may go directly to mid-off.
- A lofted shot may be caught if a fielder is positioned and can reach it.
- A shot into a genuine empty gap may travel to the boundary.
- A well-placed stroke may produce two or three runs without reaching the rope.

Do not secretly reduce a successful shot after the physical calculation just to make the game harder.

Do not secretly reward weak shots with boundaries.

## 11.3 Bowling must create dismissals other than bowled

The bowling player must have credible opportunities to dismiss the batter through:

- Bowled.
- Caught by keeper or slips.
- Caught in the ring.
- Caught by deep fielders.
- LBW.
- Stumped after advancing.
- Run out during running.

The bowler must be able to create an outside edge, top edge, mis-hit, difficult run or poor batting response through delivery choice and field placement.

