# 18. REQUIRED GAMEPLAY TEST SCENARIOS

Use reproducible test deliveries and real browser gameplay.

### Scenario A — Correct ground drive

A full delivery is bowled.

The batter chooses the correct front-foot movement and an appropriate drive direction.

Expected:
- Correct batting animation.
- Clean physical contact.
- Grounded trajectory.
- Ball travels through the actual gap.
- Fielders can still attempt to intercept.
- Runs are based on ball position and runner decisions.
- A boundary occurs only if the ball reaches the boundary.

### Scenario B — Mistimed lofted shot

The batter chooses LOFT but swings too early or late.

Expected:
- Poorer physical contact.
- Aerial mis-hit where geometry supports it.
- Ball travels towards an actual fielding position.
- Fielder tracks the ball.
- A genuine catch attempt occurs if the ball is reachable.
- Catch, drop or deflection is resolved physically.

### Scenario C — Outside edge

The bowler delivers an outswinging ball.

The batter plays a drive with an unsuitable bat-face angle or late contact.

Expected:
- Outside edge.
- Ball deflects toward the slip cordon or keeper according to contact geometry.
- Slip fielder reacts.
- Catch is possible if the fielder reaches the ball.

### Scenario D — Pull or hook

The bowler delivers a short ball.

The batter selects back foot and an appropriate cross-bat action.

Expected:
- Back-foot movement and hip rotation.
- Recognizable pull/hook swing.
- Direction depends on the actual bat angle.
- Poor timing can create a top edge or catchable ball.

### Scenario E — Yorker

Bowler aims at the base of the stumps.

Expected:
- Low full ball.
- Batter may defend, jam the bat down or miss.
- Bowled is possible.
- A successful drive should be difficult unless contact and movement genuinely support it.

### Scenario F — Ground fielding and singles

The batter pushes the ball into an infield gap.

Expected:
- The batter calls RUN.
- Both batters sprint.
- A fielder moves to the ball.
- Fielder gathers and throws.
- One run counts only after both batters ground safely.

### Scenario G — Risky second run

The batter attempts a second run.

Expected:
- Both batters turn and return.
- Fielder releases a throw to the appropriate end.
- Wicket-break timing is compared with crease-grounding time.
- Run-out is possible.

### Scenario H — Deep fielder catch

A mistimed lofted shot travels towards a deep fielder.

Expected:
- Fielder tracks the descending ball.
- Moves into a valid position.
- Attempts an appropriate catch.
- Catch is only awarded if control and applicable boundary conditions are satisfied.

### Scenario I — Correctly timed shot into a gap

The batter selects a suitable shot, footwork and direction with accurate timing.

Expected:
- Strong contact.
- Ball travels through the intended gap.
- The player is rewarded.
- Four or six depends on the actual boundary crossing.
- Two or three runs are possible through running.

### Scenario J — Multiplayer role separation

Open two independent browser clients.

Expected:
- Client A assigned BATTER sees batting controls only.
- Client B assigned BOWLER sees bowling controls only.
- Both see the same authoritative ball movement and scoreboard.
- No batting controls appear on Client B.
- No bowling controls appear on Client A.
- After innings, roles and controls swap correctly.

